      "text": "To solve the problem where \\(\\alpha\\) is inversely proportional to \\(\\beta\\), we start with the relationship given by the equation:\n\n\\[\n\\alpha \\beta = k\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(k\\) is the constant of proportionality. We are given that \\(\\alpha = 4\\) when \\(\\beta = 9\\). We can use this information to find the value of \\(k\\).\n\nFirst, substitute \\(\\alpha = 4\\) and \\(\\beta = 9\\) into the equation:\n\n\\[\n4 \\cdot 9 = k\n\\]\n\n\\[\nk = 36\n\\]\n\nNow that we have the constant \\(k\\), we can use it to find \\(\\alpha\\) when \\(\\beta = -72\\). Substitute \\(\\beta = -72\\) and \\(k = 36\\) into the equation:\n\n\\[\n\\alpha \\cdot (-72) = 36\n\\]\n\nTo solve for \\(\\alpha\\), divide both sides of the equation by \\(-72\\):\n\n\\[\n\\alpha = \\frac{36}{-72}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\alpha = -\\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(\\alpha\\) when \\(\\beta = -72\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{-\\frac{1}{2}}\n\\]",
